      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (55%):</strong> Fire pause breaks (N1 complement ~58%) and Hormuz remains closed (F4 = 5% YES, so 95% NO) while UNGA contact fails (N2 complement ~90%), sustaining and potentially broadening the kinetic exchange beyond the current Lebanon and Iran theaters.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (30%):</strong> Fire pause holds through Sep 30 (N1 = 42%) but no diplomatic breakthrough materializes (N2 = 10%, N3 = 16%), locking the conflict into an extended kinetic-economic standoff with neither side able to close the gap.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (15%):</strong> UNGA contact (N2 = 10%) or an emerging Hormuz framework (N3 = 16%) opens a negotiating track that, combined with the pause holding (N1 = 42%), permits a managed step-down in kinetic activity over the 54-day window.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · ~200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iranian state media coverage on September 21–22 centered on IRGC statements that the Hormuz restricted zone constitutes a legitimate defensive measure responding to what IRNA described as "unlawful US military aggression inside Iranian territorial sovereignty." Press TV carried multiple segments featuring Iranian legal scholars on international maritime law precedent, and the SNSC three-condition statement was broadcast as a "serious diplomatic offer" rather than a rejection of talks. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Kayhan, the hardline daily with direct ties to the Supreme Leader's office, ran a front-page editorial on September 22 arguing that any resumption of Hormuz transit without "full restoration of Iranian sovereignty rights" would constitute a capitulation, and called for continued closure as a negotiating instrument. Fars News Agency carried IRGC Navy commander statements on new mine-field enhancements in the northern Hormuz channel as a "deterrent response" to US MCM operations. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>Tasnim News Agency published a profile of President Pezeshkian's UNGA attendance, noting that his conditional September 1 offer had been "deliberately mischaracterized by Western media as a rejection." Coverage across Tasnim and IRNA on September 22 emphasized Pezeshkian's stated willingness to engage while broadcasting no information about a scheduled session or formal diplomatic channel at UNGA. No senior official statement carried by Iranian state media departed from the SNSC three-condition framework. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
      <p>The overall state-media posture as of September 22 stays anchored in defensive nationalist framing with selective openings: state outlets carried the SNSC three-condition statement as a policy document rather than a refusal, while broadcasting no official follow-on diplomatic outreach or confirmed talks timeline. The gap between official commentary on "openness" and the absence of any confirmed diplomatic channel remains the defining feature of Iranian state-media output this week. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

